(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-classes-2027q-085"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-classes-2027q-085","title":"DVRUO Classes GATE CS | Operating System | Process Synchronization | Weekly Quiz 1","sourceTitle":"DVRUO Classes GATE CS | Operating System | Process Synchronization | Weekly Quiz 1","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO CLASSES","providerKey":"classes","series":"DVRUO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027","seriesId":"cs-classes-2027q","num":85,"category":"other","year":"","format":"B","durationSec":2700,"info":{"orgName":"DVRUO Classes","batchName":"DVRUO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027","subjectName":"Operating System"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/GO CLASSES/GO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027/068_exam_992_GO Classes GATE CS Operating System Process Synchronization Weekly Quiz 1.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider $3$&nbsp;processes having synchronization primitives with counting semaphores&nbsp;$\\text{L}$ and $\\text{R}.$</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"212\" width=\"631\" src=\"media/d6/d62f57464c418b38df2d.png\"></p><p><br>Which of the following is False?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{CABACD}$ is possible output</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{CABABD}$ is possible output</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{CDABCA}$ is possible output</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{CCADBC}$ is possible output</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 7)</small></h4><a name=\"410784\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Given L=3, R=0.</p>\n\n<p>Let’s check options one by one.</p>\n\n<p>Since these are infinite executions, we need to check the given sequence till that point is valid or not</p>\n\n<p>Option A –&nbsp;CABACD:</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Execute P1 –&nbsp;prints “C”.( L=2, R=1). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“C”</li>\n\t<li>Execute P2 –&nbsp;prints A,B (L=2, R=1). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“CAB”.</li>\n\t<li>Execute P2 –&nbsp;print A and then preempt&nbsp;(L=2, R=0). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“CABA”.</li>\n\t<li>Execute P1 –&nbsp;prints “C”.( L=1, R=1). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“CABAC”.</li>\n\t<li>Execute P3&nbsp;–&nbsp;prints “D”.( L=1, R=0). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“CABACD”</li>\n\t<li>So VALID.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Option B&nbsp;–&nbsp;CABABD:</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Execute P1 –&nbsp;prints “C”.( L=2, R=1). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“C”</li>\n\t<li>Execute P2 –&nbsp;prints A,B (L=2, R=1). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“CAB”.</li>\n\t<li>Execute P2 –&nbsp;prints&nbsp;A,B.(L=2, R=1). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“CABAB”.</li>\n\t<li>Execute P3&nbsp;–&nbsp;prints “D”.( L=2, R=0). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“CABABD”</li>\n\t<li>So VALID.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Option C&nbsp;–&nbsp;CDABCA:</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Execute P1 –&nbsp;prints “C”.( L=2, R=1). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“C”</li>\n\t<li>Execute P3&nbsp;–&nbsp;prints “D”.( L=2, R=0). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“CD”</li>\n\t<li>Since R=0, we can’t execute P2 directly. We can make R=1 by executing P1 again. But it prints C&nbsp;before making R=1. Then, the sequence looks like “CDC..” Hnece we can’t produce sequence as “CDAB..”.</li>\n\t<li>So INVALID.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Option D –&nbsp;CCADBC</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Execute P1 –&nbsp;prints “C”.( L=2, R=1). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“C”</li>\n\t<li>Execute P1 –&nbsp;prints “C”.( L=1, R=2). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“CC”</li>\n\t<li>Execute P2 –&nbsp;print A and then preempt&nbsp;(L=1, R=1). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“CCA”.</li>\n\t<li>Execute P3&nbsp;–&nbsp;prints “D”.( L=1, R=0). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“CCAD”</li>\n\t<li>Execute P2 remaining part –&nbsp;print B (L=1, R=1). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“CCADB”.</li>\n\t<li>Execute P1 –&nbsp;prints “C”.( L=0, R=1). So sequence till now –&nbsp;“CCADBC”</li>\n\t<li>So VALID</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410210\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":2,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>The notorious dining gourmand problems is as follows:</p><p>Six gourmands are seated around a table, with a substantial portion of perfectly cooked roast vegetables at the center.<br>Forks $\\text{(represented as ‘F’)}$ and knives $\\text{(represented as ‘K’)}$ are arranged as shown below. (Gourmands who prefer their roasted vegetables less cooked wear displeased expressions.) Each gourmand obeys the following algorithm:</p><ol><li>Grab the closest knife.</li><li>Grab the closest fork.</li><li>Eat roasted vegetables</li><li>Put down the knife and fork where they used to be</li></ol><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"288\" width=\"357\" src=\"media/ca/ca89ed0fad9dba2b084e.png\"></p><p>Which statement(s) among the following is/are TRUE regarding the dining gourmand problem presented above?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>The possibility of deadlock exists in the given scenario.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The possibility of deadlock does NOT exist in the given scenario.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Deadlock can occur if we swap steps $1$ and $2$ in the algorithm for odd philosophers while keeping the algorithm unchanged for even philosophers.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Deadlock can occur if we swap steps $1$ and $2$&nbsp;in the algorithm for even philosophers while keeping the algorithm unchanged for odd philosophers.</p>"}],"answer":["B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 6)</small></h4><a name=\"410312\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>In the given scenario, there is no possibility of Deadlock since the condition of Circular&nbsp;Wait for Deadlock is not satisfied. In simple words,&nbsp;you will not get&nbsp;a situation where each gourmand is holding a knife, waiting for a fork to become available&nbsp;and no one can proceed.</p>\n\n<p>If you swap steps 1 and 2 for odd gourmands (meaning they first grab a fork and then a knife) while keeping the algorithm unchanged for even gourmands, you can have a situation where all odd gourmands are waiting for a knife to become available, and all even&nbsp;gourmands are waiting for a fork to become available, leading to a deadlock.</p>\n\n<p>Similarly you can figure out what will happen if you swap&nbsp;steps&nbsp;1&nbsp;and&nbsp;2&nbsp;in the algorithm for even philosophers while keeping the algorithm unchanged for odd philosophers.</p>\n\n<p><u><strong>So the correct options are B,C,D.</strong></u></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"498829\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">For option C and D<br>\n<br>\nC: first take odd wale ka &nbsp;fork and then preempt<br>\n<br>\nthen take even wale ka knife and preempt now that is a situation of deadlock<br>\n<br>\nsame as opotin D</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"500424\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0\"><b>Correct Statements: B, C, and D</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"4\">This is a variation of the Dining Philosophers problem, but with a specific resource layout (Knives <code>K</code> and Forks <code>F</code> alternating) and a specific algorithm (Knife first, then Fork).</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"5\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,0\"><b>Why B is True (No Deadlock initially):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1,0,0\">The algorithm requires everyone to grab a Knife first.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1,1,0\">Looking at the table, Knives are shared between pairs (e.g., Gourmands 1 and 2 share a knife; 3 and 4 share a knife; 5 and 6 share a knife).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1,2,0\">This creates a competition at Step 1. Only one person in each pair can win the knife. For example, if Gourmand 1 grabs the knife, Gourmand 2 is blocked and holds nothing.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1,3,0\">The \"winners\" of Step 1 (e.g., 1, 3, 5) then proceed to Step 2: Grabbing a Fork.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1,4,0\">The forks are located between the pairs (e.g., F is between 6 and 1). Since the neighbor (6) likely lost their knife fight or is engaged elsewhere, the fork is generally uncontested for the winner. Even if two winners compete for a fork, one will get it, eat, and release.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1,5,0\"><b>Crucially</b>, deadlock requires a \"Circular Wait\" where everyone holds a resource and waits for another. Here, the \"losers\" of the knife fight hold nothing, so they cannot be part of a deadlock. The \"winners\" can proceed. Thus, deadlock does not exist.</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,0\"><b>Why C and D are True (Swapping causes Deadlock):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1,0,0\">Standard deadlock prevention often involves making adjacent philosophers use different strategies (asymmetry). However, in this specific layout, introducing that asymmetry actually <b>creates</b> the cycle.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1,1,0\"><b>Scenario C (Odds swap):</b> Odd gourmands (1, 3, 5) grab Fork first. Even gourmands (2, 4, 6) grab Knife first.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1,1,1,0,0\">Gourmand 1 grabs Fork (to their left).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1,1,1,1,0\">Gourmand 2 grabs Knife (to their left).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1,1,1,2,0\">... and so on. Everyone successfully grabs their \"left-hand\" item in Step 1.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1,1,1,3,0\">Now, everyone wants their \"right-hand\" item for Step 2. But that item is held by their neighbor to the right.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1,1,1,4,0\">This forms a perfect dependency cycle ($1 \\to 2 \\to 3 \\to 4 \\to 5 \\to 6 \\to 1$). <b>Deadlock occurs.</b></p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1,2,0\"><b>Scenario D (Evens swap):</b> The same logic applies, just creating the cycle in the reverse direction</p></li></ul></li></ul></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"538931\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>answer is B,C,D<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/30/30c6ba9b246f09442e9e.png\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2c/2c9cb02f8dc0743a3de6.png\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539634\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><ul data-path-to-node=\"0\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Odd Gourmands (1, 3, 5):</b> Grab Knife (Right) first, then Fork (Left).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Even Gourmands (2, 4, 6):</b> Grab Knife (Left) first, then Fork (Right).</p></li></ul><ul data-path-to-node=\"2\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"2,0,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"2,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Why A is False &amp; B is True (No Deadlock initially):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"2,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"2,0,1,0,0\">Adjacent gourmands (1-2, 3-4, 5-6) compete for the exact same knife between them in Step 1.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"2,0,1,1,0\">Only one from each pair gets the knife; the other is immediately blocked at Step 1.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"2,0,1,2,0\">The 3 winners proceed to Step 2 and find all 3 forks completely untouched and free. They eat, finish, and prevent any circular wait from forming.</p></li></ul></li></ul><ul data-path-to-node=\"5\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"5,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Why C is True (Deadlock via Odd Swap):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1,0,0\">Swapping steps for odd gourmands means they grab Forks first (Left).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1,1,0\">Even gourmands continue to grab Knives first (Left).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1,2,0\">Every gourmand successfully grabs one resource in Step 1, leaving the table empty and forcing a total circular wait deadlock at Step 2.</p></li></ul></li></ul><ul data-path-to-node=\"7\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"7,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Why D is True (Deadlock via Even Swap):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"7,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,1,0,0\">Swapping steps for even gourmands causes them to pick up Forks first.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,1,1,0\">Odd gourmands simultaneously pick up Knives first.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,1,2,0\">All resources are instantly taken in Step 1, creating a symmetric circular wait deadlock at Step 2.</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"7,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Correct Options: B, C, and D</b></p></li></ul></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410209\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>A parking can accommodate up to $20$ cars. There is one gate through which all cars must go one at a time when they enter or leave the parking.<br>Add the required semaphore calls to the following template to ensure the conditions:</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:lower-alpha\"><li>The parking will never contain more than $20$ cars, and</li><li>Cars entering or leaving the parking will go through the gate one at a time.</li></ol><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">semaphore spaces = 20;\nsemaphore gate = 1;\nenter_parking()\n{ \n    X: \n    Y: \n    go_through_gate(); \n    V(gate);\n}// enter_parking \n\nleave_parking()\n{ \n    P(gate);\n    go_through_gate(); \n    V(gate);\n    Z: \n}// leave_parking</pre><p>Fill the terms in place of X, Y, and Z such that given conditions&nbsp;a, and b are satisfied.<br>“Blank” in the options represents no term needed.</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{X: P(spaces), Y:  P(gate), Z: V(spaces)}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{X: P(gate), Y: Blank, Z: V(spaces)}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{X: P(spaces), Y: V(spaces), Z: V(gate)}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{X: Blank, Y: P(spaces), Z: V(gate)}$</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"410315\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>X: <strong>P(spaces)</strong> - This semaphore call is used to decrement the available parking spaces when a car enters the parking lot. It ensures that the parking will never contain more than 20 cars.</p>\n\n<p>Y: <strong>P(gate)</strong> - This semaphore call is used to acquire the gate semaphore, which allows only one car to go through the gate at a time when entering or leaving the parking. You can think of this gate as a Critical Section wherein atmost 1 process(car) will be allowed to pass through at any time.</p>\n\n<p>Z: <strong>V(spaces)</strong> - This semaphore call is used to increment the available parking spaces when a car leaves the parking lot.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><u><strong>Answer is option A.</strong></u></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"482741\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Try by eliminating options.</p><p>Start with&nbsp;<em>leave_parking()</em>&nbsp;since it has only one blank space.&nbsp;</p><p>On analyzing option we can see either it can be <em>V(spaces)</em> or <em>V(gate)</em>. Since there is just one gate, it's value can never be greater than 1 (else two cars can aquire it and collide).&nbsp;</p><p>In the <em>leave_parking()</em> function, <em>V(gate)</em> is already performed which indicates that gate is now open for other cars so adding <em>V(gate)</em> again won't make much sense, hence the only option left is <em>V(spaces)</em> and it make sense as well, that we should increment the available parking space when the car leaves.</p><p><strong>Hence we can eliminate option C and D since they have Z: V(gate) as one of the operations.</strong></p><p>Now, we also know that while using gate we must have a lock over it and since we are also occupying the parking space we should decrement the space count while entering. i.e. we need to perform <em>P(spaces)</em> and <em>P(gate)</em> both for safe operation. <strong>Option B can be eliminated with this logic, since it doesn't decrement the available spaces.</strong></p><p><strong>Hence option A is correct.</strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539732\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\"><span style=\"background-color:#ffff99;\">This problem is&nbsp; using two types of semaphores: a counting semaphore to track resource availability and a binary semaphore (mutex) to enforce mutual exclusion.</span></p><p>The counting semaphore <code data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"23\">spaces</code> initializes to 20 to limit the maximum capacity, ensuring that cars can only enter if a free slot exists, while the binary semaphore <code data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"163\">gate</code> initializes to 1 to ensure that only one car passes through the single entryway at any given point in time.</p><p>When a car wants to enter the lot via <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"38\">enter_parking()</code>, it must first check if a slot is vacant by calling a wait operation on the capacity counter. This fills placeholder X with <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"178\">P(spaces)</code>.</p><p>Once a slot is successfully acquired, the car needs to pass through the entryway. It secures exclusive access to the pathway by checking the gate mutex, which fills placeholder Y with <code data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"184\">P(gate)</code>.</p><p>Looking at the provided code for <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"33\">enter_parking()</code>, the system already safely executes <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"85\">go_through_gate()</code> and releases the gate right after with <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"142\">V(gate)</code>.</p><p>When a car leaves the lot via <code data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"30\">leave_parking()</code>, it acquires the gate mutex via <code data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"78\">P(gate)</code>, moves through the gate via <code data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"114\">go_through_gate()</code>, and releases it via <code data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"153\">V(gate)</code>.</p><p>After exiting the gate, the car frees up a parking slot for future vehicles by executing a signal operation to increment the capacity tracker, which fills placeholder Z with <code data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\" data-index-in-node=\"174\">V(spaces)</code>.</p><p>Putting this all together gives us the correct sequence: X is <code data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\" data-index-in-node=\"62\">P(spaces)</code>, Y is <code data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\" data-index-in-node=\"78\">P(gate)</code>, and Z is <code data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\" data-index-in-node=\"96\">V(spaces)</code>.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,8,0\">Correct Option: A</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410208\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":4,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following are true about semaphores?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Semaphore $\\text{V()}$ increments the value of the semaphore and wakes a sleeping thread if the value of the semaphore is $&lt;= 0$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Semaphores can NOT be used for locking.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The interface for Semaphore $\\text{P()}$ is specified in a way that prevents its implementation from busy waiting, even for a brief period of time.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The pure semaphore interface does not allow querying for the current value of the semaphore.</p>"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"500425\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"8\"><b>Correct Statements: A and D</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,0,0\"><b>A. Semaphore V() increments... (TRUE):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,0,1,0,0\">This statement accurately describes the standard implementation of a <b>blocking semaphore</b> (often called the \"counting semaphore with negative values\").</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,0,1,1,0\">In this model, the semaphore value tracks the count: positive means available resources, negative means waiting threads.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,0,1,2,0\">When <code>V()</code> (Signal) is called, it increments the value. If the result is still non-positive (e.g., goes from -1 to 0), it implies there was a thread waiting, so it wakes one up.</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,1,0\"><b>B. Semaphores can NOT be used for locking (FALSE):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,1,1,0,0\">Binary semaphores (initialized to 1) are widely used as locks (Mutexes).</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,2,0\"><b>C. The interface prevents busy waiting... (FALSE):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9,2,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,2,1,0,0\">The semaphore <i>interface</i> (P and V operations) defines <i>what</i> happens, not <i>how</i>. While they are designed to allow blocking (sleeping), they do not strictly prevent an implementation that uses busy-waiting (like spinlocks), nor do they prevent the internal implementation of the semaphore itself from using brief spinlocks for atomicity.</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,3,0\"><b>D. The pure semaphore interface does not allow querying... (TRUE):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9,3,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,3,1,0,0\">In Dijkstra's original definition (the \"pure\" interface), the semaphore is an opaque data type. The only allowed operations are P (Wait) and V (Signal). You cannot inspect the integer value directly because doing so usually leads to race conditions (e.g., checking <code>if (S &gt; 0)</code> before waiting is unsafe).</p></li></ul></li></ul></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"412576\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>A. <strong>True.</strong> Semaphore V() increments the value of the semaphore and wakes a sleeping thread if the value of the semaphore is &lt;=0.</p>\n\n<p>B. False. Semaphores can be used for locking. For example, a semaphore can be used to lock a shared resource, such as a file, so that only one thread can access it at a time.</p>\n\n<p>C. False. The interface for Semaphore P() is not specified in a way that prevents its implementation from busywaiting. Some implementations of Semaphore P() may busywait for a brief period of time.</p>\n\n<p>D. <strong>True.</strong> The pure semaphore interface does not allow querying for the current value of the semaphore.</p>\n\n<p>So the answer is A, D</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539734\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\">CONCEPT TOUCHES:</span></p><p>A semaphore is basically a protected variable that helps different threads or processes communicate and synchronize their actions so they do not crash into each other or cause race conditions.</p><p>The two main operations used here are $P()$—which acts as a wait or acquire check—and $V()$—which acts as a signal or release check.</p><p>&nbsp;In a standard setup, when a process calls $P()$, it decrements the semaphore value; if that value drops below zero, the process is put to sleep in a waiting queue. When a process finishes its task, it calls $V()$ to increment the value and wake up a sleeping process from that queue if there is one.</p><p>This problem also revolves around the use of busy-waiting&nbsp; in process synchronization, and how they interact with semaphores. Busy-waiting, also known as spinning, is a technique where a process repeatedly executes a loop checking a condition (like waiting for a lock to open) without giving up the CPU.&nbsp;</p><p>This wastes massive amounts of processor cycles because the CPU is working hard doing nothing useful.</p><p>&nbsp;To solve this efficiency problem, modern operating systems use a block-sleep mechanism: when a process cannot get a resource, the kernel changes its state to \"blocked,\" moves it into a waiting queue, and frees up the CPU for other productive tasks.</p><p>&nbsp;When the resource becomes available, a wake-up signal moves the blocked process back to the ready queue.</p><hr><ul><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\">Option A is true because a counter value of 0 or less shows that the resource is tapped out and threads are sleeping in the queue. Calling $V()$ bumps the count up and immediately tells the operating system to wake up one of those sleeping threads.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\">Option B is false because you can easily use a binary semaphore as a lock by starting it at 1. The first process to call $P()$ drops it to 0 and walks in, forcing any other process that arrives later to wait outside until the first one calls $V()$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\">Option C is false because even though semaphores save CPU time by sleeping threads, the inner code for $P()$ and $V()$ has to be completely safe from split-second race conditions. To update the shared number safely on multi-core chips, the kernel has to use tiny internal spinlocks, causing a split-second of busy-waiting.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\">Option D is true because the standard semaphore design completely hides the internal counter number from the regular programmer. You are only allowed to initialize, wait with $P()$, or signal with $V()$, which stops people from writing broken code based on reading unstable numbers.</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\">Correct Options: A and D</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410207\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":5,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose a program has three threads $\\textsf{Thread1}, \\textsf{Thread2}$ and $\\textsf{Thread3}$ and a shared counter, count, as shown below:</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">int count = 10;\n\nThread1(...) \n{ // do something \n    Wait(Lock); \n    count++; \n    Signal(Lock); \n}\n\nThread2(...) \n{ // do something \n    Wait(Lock); \n    count --; \n    Signal(Lock); \n}\n\nThread3(...) \n{ // do something \n    Wait(Lock); \n    print(count);\n    Signal(Lock); \n}</pre><p>&nbsp;</p><p>What is the possible output that get printed after the completion of all threads? (Mark all the outputs which are possible)</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$8$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$9$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$10$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$11$</p>"}],"answer":["B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, C, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"473348\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-start=\"103\" data-end=\"128\">You have three threads:</p><ul data-start=\"129\" data-end=\"226\"><li data-start=\"129\" data-end=\"162\"><p data-start=\"131\" data-end=\"162\">Thread1&nbsp;increments <code data-start=\"154\" data-end=\"161\">count</code>.</p></li><li data-start=\"163\" data-end=\"196\"><p data-start=\"165\" data-end=\"196\">Thread2 decrements <code data-start=\"188\" data-end=\"195\">count</code>.</p></li><li data-start=\"197\" data-end=\"226\"><p data-start=\"199\" data-end=\"226\">Thread3 prints <code data-start=\"218\" data-end=\"225\">count</code></p></li></ul><p><strong>Since,All the operations within the critical sections no race condition will happen and only the interleaving order can only affects the output.</strong></p><p>Let's lookout possible execution orders of three threads :&nbsp;</p><p>i.e since,each thread runs exactly once no thread is repeated so, the no,, of permuatations of three thread is 3!=6.</p><p>Initial value of count is 10</p><p>1.Thread1 --&gt; Thread2 --&gt; Thread3</p><p>count=11 --&gt; count=10 --&gt; prints 10</p><p>2.Thread2 --&gt; Thread1 --&gt; Thread3</p><p>&nbsp; count=9 --&gt; count=10 --&gt; prints 10</p><p>3.Thread1 --&gt; Thread3 --&gt; Thread2</p><p>&nbsp; count=11 --&gt; prints 11 --&gt; count=10</p><p>4.Thread2 --&gt; Thread3 --&gt; Thread1</p><p>&nbsp; count=9 --&gt; prints 9&nbsp; --&gt; count=10</p><p>5.Thread3 --&gt; Thread2 --&gt; Thread1</p><p>&nbsp; prints 10 --&gt; count=9&nbsp; --&gt; count=10</p><p>6.Thread3 --&gt; Thread1 --&gt; Thread2</p><p>&nbsp; prints 10 --&gt; count=11&nbsp; --&gt; count=10</p><p>Therefore, possible outputs are 9,10,11&nbsp;</p><p>so, c<strong>orrect Answers are B,C,D</strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"492441\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><div style=\"font-family:'Segoe UI', sans-serif; font-size:17px; line-height:1.8; color:#222;\"><p>Notice that there is no <strong><code>while</code></strong> loop in any thread. Hence, each thread executes exactly once, it performs its operation and exits.</p><p>Because of the lock, only one thread can execute its critical section at a time. This ensures that all operations on <strong><code>count</code></strong> happen atomically, and there is no race condition.</p><p>The value printed depends on when <strong><code>Thread 3</code></strong> executes relative to the other two threads:</p><ul style=\"margin-top:8px;\"><li>If <strong>Thread 3</strong> runs before both → it prints 10.</li><li>If it runs after <strong>Thread 1</strong> (and before Thread 2) → it prints 11.</li><li>If it runs after <strong>Thread 2</strong> (and before Thread 1) → it prints 9.</li><li>If it runs after both Thread 1 and Thread 2 → it prints 10 again.</li></ul><p>Since each thread executes only once, these are the only possible scenarios.</p><h3 style=\"margin-top:16px;\">Possible outputs</h3><p>9, 10, and 11</p><h3 style=\"margin-top:16px;\">&nbsp;</h3></div></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539736\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\">A critical section is a block of code that accesses shared variables and <span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\">must not be run by more than one thread at the same time to prevent messy race conditions.&nbsp;</span></p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\">Locks handle this by forcing threads to call a wait or acquire operation before entering, ensuring that only one thread holds the key while others wait outside until a signal or release operation opens it up again.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\">Because every single thread (Thread1, Thread2, and Thread3) wraps its entire data operation perfectly inside <code data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"109\">Wait(Lock)</code> and <code data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"124\">Signal(Lock)</code>, the scheduler is forced to execute their critical section operations one at a time in a strictly serialized manner.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\">The shared variable starts at a fixed baseline value:</p><p>$$\\text{count} = 10$$</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\">If Thread1 runs its critical section first, it makes the counter up:</p><p>$$\\text{count} = 10 + 1 = 11$$</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\">If Thread2 runs its critical section first, it drops the counter down:</p><p>$$\\text{count} = 10 - 1 = 9$$</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\">thread3 can execute its print statement at three distinct milestones.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\"><strong>Case 1:</strong> Thread3 executes first before any math happens, printing the initial baseline value of 10. Then Thread1 and Thread2 execute in any order, a final variable value of 10.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\"><strong>Case 2: </strong>Thread1 executes first to make the value to 11, and then Thread3 runs right after, printing the value 11. Finally, Thread2 runs to bring the end count variable down to 10.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,8,0\"><strong>Case 3: </strong>Thread2 executes first to drop the value to 9, and then Thread3 runs right after, printing the value 9. Finally, Thread1 runs to bring the end count variable back up to 10.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,8,0\">10 will be the final value at end in every case but that doesnot matter. Because question ask on the possibility of values that will get printed.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,9,0\">Because the print statement inside Thread3 is perfectly isolated inside its own atomic lock block, it can only see and print the intermediate values 9, 10, or 11, making it mathematically impossible to ever see or print 8.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,10,0\">Correct Options: B, C, and D</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410206\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":6,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>In the given implementation of a critical section, where $\\textsf{'mutex'}$ is a binary semaphore initialized as $1$ and $\\textsf{'x'}$ is a shared integer initialized as $0.$</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">down (mutex);\nif (x == 0) {\n    up (mutex);\n    down (mutex);\n    x++;\n}\nup (mutex);</pre><p>What is the maximum possible final value of&nbsp;$\\textsf{'x'}$ when $10$ threads are concurrently executing the same code?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[10,10],"answerText":"10","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"440474\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p dir=\"ltr\"><b id=\"docs-internal-guid-50695c58-7fff-3560-dbb9-15555f74f3a9\">Every process will execute this V(m) inside \"if\" and will get preempted. Then every process will be at stmt P(m). Finally one process will execute P(m),x++,V(m). So after executing V(m) outside \"if\" other process can continue same. Hence at last x=10.</b></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"500421\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"8\"><b>Answer: 10</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"9\">This problem highlights a classic <b>Race Condition</b> caused by releasing a lock prematurely.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"10\"><b>The Goal:</b> We want to maximize the final value of <code>x</code>. This means we want as many threads as possible to enter the <code>if (x == 0)</code> block and execute the increment <code>x++</code>.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"11\"><b>The Vulnerability:</b> Notice lines 1-3:</p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"12\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,0,0\"><code>down(mutex);</code></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,1,0\"><code>if (x == 0) {</code></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,2,0\"><code>up(mutex);</code> &lt;-- <b>DANGER!</b></p></li></ol><p data-path-to-node=\"13\">The thread checks the condition <code>x==0</code> while holding the lock, but then <b>releases the lock</b> before doing the work. This opens a \"window of opportunity\" for other threads to sneak in.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"14\"><b>The Worst-Case Trace (Maximizing x):</b></p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"15\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,0,0\"><b>Thread 1</b> runs. It acquires the lock, checks <code>x</code> (which is 0), and enters the <code>if</code> block. It executes <code>up(mutex)</code> and is then preempted (paused) right before the second <code>down(mutex)</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,1,0\"><b>Thread 2</b> runs. The lock is free (because Thread 1 released it). Thread 2 checks <code>x</code>. Since Thread 1 hasn't incremented it yet, <code>x</code> is still 0. Thread 2 enters the <code>if</code> block, releases the lock, and is preempted.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,2,0\"><b>Threads 3 through 10</b> repeat this exact process. Because no one has actually reached the <code>x++</code> line yet, <code>x</code> remains 0. All 10 threads successfully pass the check and stack up inside the <code>if</code> block.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,3,0\"><b>The Increments:</b> Now, the threads resume one by one.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"15,3,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,3,1,0,0\">Thread 1 grabs the lock at line 4, increments <code>x</code> to 1, and releases.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,3,1,1,0\">Thread 2 grabs the lock at line 4, increments <code>x</code> to 2, and releases.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,3,1,2,0\">...</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,3,1,3,0\">Thread 10 grabs the lock at line 4, increments <code>x</code> to 10, and releases.</p></li></ul></li></ol><p data-path-to-node=\"16\"><b>Final Value:</b> Since all 10 threads were \"tricked\" into entering the increment block, the final value is <b>10</b>.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"500746\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>10 processes are there ; mutex =1 ; x=0. Now just read the below working :</p><p>1st process arrives -&gt;</p><p>1. decrements the mutex</p><p>2. checks if(x==0) and enters inisde the if condition.</p><p>3. increment the mutex.</p><p><strong>At this point 1st process pre-empts</strong>. Now we have <strong>mutex =1 and x is still 0.</strong></p><p>2nd process arrives , we have mutex =1 -&gt;</p><p>1. decrements the mutex</p><p>2. checks if(x==0) and enters inisde the if condition.</p><p>3. increment the mutex.</p><p>At this point<strong> 2nd process pre-empts.</strong> Now we have <strong>mutex =1.</strong></p><p>Same procedure happens for all 10 processes and when we execute the last process, we have mutex =1 after following the above three steps.</p><p>From here onwards we can do these three operations:</p><p>1. decrements the mutex</p><p>2. increment x by 1.</p><p>3. increment the mutex.</p><p>each process executes this and finally we get x= 10 after getting incremented by 10 processes.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>I hope this solution helps. :)</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539738\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\"><span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\">This problem centers on race conditions&nbsp; in multi-threaded programming.&nbsp;</span></p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\">A race condition happens when the final outcome of concurrent execution depends on the specific orderf thread interleaving.&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\">For a lock to protect a shared variable perfectly, the entire sequence of checking a condition and modifying the variable must be atomic, meaning no other thread can sneak in and read the old state mid-way through the process.&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\">The system initializes with a clear starting point where the lock variable and the shared integer are set to:</p><p>$$\\text{mutex} = 1, \\quad x = 0$$</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\">When thread 1 arrives, it calls <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"60\">down(mutex)</code> to enter, passes the <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"93\">if (x == 0)</code> check, but immediately calls <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"134\">up(mutex)</code> to release the lock before it ever touches or increments $x$.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\">Because the thread releases the lock right after passing the check, any other thread can freely grab the lock, read that $x$ is still 0, pass the same condition check, and release the lock as well.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\">We can create a worst-case scheduling sequence where Thread 1 enters, confirms $x == 0$, executes <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"96\">up(mutex)</code>, and gets preempted right before running the second <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"158\">down(mutex)</code> statement on line 4.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\">The CPU scheduler can repeat this exact sequence for Thread 2, Thread 3, all the way through Thread 10, allowing every single one of them to successfully pass the conditional check while $x$ is still sitting at 0.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\">At this specific moment, all 10 threads are lined up right at line 4, waiting to execute their individual second <code data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\" data-index-in-node=\"113\">down(mutex)</code> operations.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\">One by one, each thread will cleanly acquire the lock, increment the counter via <code data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\" data-index-in-node=\"81\">x++</code>, and release the lock via the final <code data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\" data-index-in-node=\"121\">up(mutex)</code> statement.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,8,0\">Since all 10 threads already successfully bypassed when $x$ was 0, every single one of them will perform exactly one increment operation regardless of the new value of $x$.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,9,0\">This means $x$ will be incremented a total of 10 times sequentially</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,10,0\">Final Answer: 10</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,10,0\"><span style=\"background-color:#2ecc71;\">follow up:</span></p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,10,0\">min value? it is 1</p><p>If Thread 1 finishes completely first, it sets $x = 1$. When threads 2 through 10 wake up later, they fail the initial <code data-path-to-node=\"0,12,0\" data-index-in-node=\"117\">if (x == 0)</code> condition entirely, skip the inner block, and exit without changing anything, which guarantees the final value stays at 1.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"543836\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><blockquote><p>I did not find atleast one valid explanation then I thought from C - Programming&nbsp; Prespective and coA prespective&nbsp;</p><p>Threads PRST = prgram couter, register set , stack , thread id can't share and gloal variable&nbsp; is stored in static memory ..</p><p>Now come to question we have 10 different Threads it read and goes to if staement now every thread increments and then come back and final value becomes zero.&nbsp;</p><p>But u have still dout here we already read value right into instruction set architecture but here they are not mentioned RISC architectre&nbsp; and also here instruction completed in line 2 . then it enter into 3 line now it reads new instruction .&nbsp;</p><p>they read used for comparison it is completed instruction there it self why u come to mentioning after 2 instruction also .</p></blockquote><p><strong>It is blunder bro .&nbsp;</strong></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410205\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following is/are TRUE?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Once a thread has acquired a lock $\\text{(using P()),}$ it cannot be preempted by the scheduler until it has released the lock by calling $\\text{V()}.$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Peterson’s algorithm can be used to enforce mutual exclusion on a multiprocessor.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Peterson’s algorithm assumes the existence of a test-and-set instruction.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>In a uniprocessor environment, when multiple threads exist within a single process, synchronization among the threads is unnecessary, even if they access shared data.</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 6)</small></h4><a name=\"412577\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>A. Once a thread has acquired a lock&nbsp;(using P()),it cannot be preempted by the scheduler until it has released the lock by calling&nbsp;V()</strong></p>\n\n<p>False. Once a thread has acquired a lock, it can still be preempted by the scheduler. This means that another thread can be scheduled to run, even though the first thread still holds the lock. When the first thread is rescheduled, it will be able to continue executing from where it left off, and it will still hold the lock.</p>\n\n<p><strong>B. Peterson’s algorithm can be used to enforce mutual exclusion on a multiprocessor.</strong></p>\n\n<p>True. Peterson's algorithm is a mutual exclusion algorithm that can be used on multiprocessor systems. It works by using shared memory to coordinate the threads.</p>\n\n<p><strong>C. Peterson’s algorithm assumes the existence of a test-and-set instruction.</strong></p>\n\n<p>False. Peterson's algorithm does not assume the existence of a test-and-set instruction. It can be implemented using only atomic operations such as read and write.</p>\n\n<p><strong>D. In a uniprocessor environment, when multiple threads exist within a single process, synchronization among the threads is unnecessary, even if they access shared data.</strong></p>\n\n<p>False. Even in a uniprocessor environment, synchronization among threads is necessary if they are accessing shared data. If two threads access the same shared data at the same time without synchronization, it can lead to data races and other errors.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, the only true statement is B. Peterson's algorithm can be used to enforce mutual exclusion on a multiprocessor.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539743\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\"><span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\">Peterson's algorithm is built to handle mutual exclusion purely through software, using basic shared variables like flags and turn counters.</span></p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\">The operating system scheduler can still pause a thread at any random moment, which means threads can still mess up shared data even on a single CPU core.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\">Option A is false because getting a lock stops other threads from entering the critical section, but it does not stop the CPU from pausing your thread to let someone else run.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\">Option B is true because the logic of Peterson's algorithm works fine on multiple processors, as long as the two threads can read and write to the same shared memory.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\">Option C is false because the whole point of Peterson's algorithm is that it is a pure software trick, meaning it does not need special hardware tools like a test-and-set instruction.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\">Option D is false because a single CPU core uses time-sharing to jump back and forth between threads, which will corrupt shared data if we do not use synchronization.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\">Correct Option: B</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410204\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":8,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider a concurrent program with many threads. There are two types of threads. Type A threads repeatedly call ProcedureA, shown below. Type B threads repeatedly call ProcedureB. These two procedures are synchronized using three semaphores, semX, semY, and semZ. You should assume that semX and semY each have an initial value of $1,$ and that semZ has an initial value of $2.$&nbsp;ProcedureA and ProcedureB also make calls to ProcedureX, ProcedureY, and ProcedureZ. These procedures are not shown. However, they do not make any use of synchronization primitives.</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"322\" width=\"662\" src=\"media/e1/e1b6a8ece05be7349993.png\"></p><p><br>Which of the following is/are TRUE?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>It is possible that two threads can execute ProcedureX() concurrently</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>It is possible that some thread will execute ProcedureX() at the same time that another thread is executing ProcedureY()</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>It is possible that some Type A thread will execute ProcedureX() at the same time that another Type A thread is executing ProcedureZ()</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>It possible that a deadlock will occur among these threads?</p>"}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"473350\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Initial values for semx=1,semy=1,semz=2</p><p><strong>A. It is possible that two threads can execute ProcedureX() concurrently</strong></p><p><strong>No</strong></p><p>since,semaphore semx provides mutual exclusion to access procedurex().so,it is not possible that two thread can&nbsp; execute procedurex() concurrenlty<strong> </strong></p><p><strong>B. It is possible that some thread will execute ProcedureX() at the same time that another thread is executing ProcedureY()</strong></p><p><strong>Yes</strong></p><p>since,mutual exclusion has been provided to procedurex() by semaphore semx and for procedurey() by semaphore semy so,that two different threads can execute procedurex() and procedurey() concurrently.</p><p><strong>C. It is possible that some Type A thread will execute ProcedureX() at the same time that another Type A thread is executing ProcedureZ()</strong></p><p><strong>yes</strong></p><p>if TypeA thread will execute procedurex()&nbsp; then semaphore semx becomes&nbsp; 0 then right after v(semx) it again becomes 1 now,first typeA thread is currenlty executing the Procedurez() Now,second typeA thread starts execution can execute procedurex() and still previous TypeA thread is executing procedurez().</p><p><strong>D. It possible that a deadlock will occur among these threads?</strong></p><p><strong>No</strong></p><p>deadlock is not possible because,simulataneously start both the procedureA() and procedureB() now,you can check that at any statement atleast one of them is being executed.There is no possibility that both procedureA() and procedureB() will be blocked.&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Therefore,&nbsp;</strong></p><p><strong>B,C are the final correct answers</strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"500420\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"3\"><b>Correct Statements: B and C</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"4\">Let's analyze each option based on the provided code structure.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"5\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,0\"><b>A. It is possible that two threads can execute ProcedureX() concurrently. (FALSE)</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1,0,0\"><code>ProcedureX()</code> is guarded immediately by <code>P(semX)</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1,1,0\">Since <code>semX</code> is initialized to <b>1</b>, it acts as a <b>mutex</b> (mutual exclusion lock).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,1,2,0\">Only one thread can pass <code>P(semX)</code> at a time. Any other thread trying to enter will be blocked until the first thread executes <code>V(semX)</code>. Therefore, concurrency inside <code>ProcedureX</code> is impossible.</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,0\"><b>B. It is possible that some thread will execute ProcedureX() at the same time that another thread is executing ProcedureY(). (TRUE)</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1,0,0\"><code>ProcedureX()</code> is guarded by <code>semX</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1,1,0\"><code>ProcedureY()</code> is guarded by <code>semY</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1,2,0\">These are two completely independent semaphores.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,1,3,0\">A Type A thread can acquire <code>semX</code> and enter <code>ProcedureX()</code>. Concurrently, a Type B thread can acquire <code>semY</code> and enter <code>ProcedureY()</code>. There is no dependency preventing this parallel execution.</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,2,0\"><b>C. It is possible that some Type A thread will execute ProcedureX() at the same time that another Type A thread is executing ProcedureZ(). (TRUE)</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"5,2,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,2,1,0,0\">Let's trace the execution flow of a Type A thread (Thread 1):</p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"5,2,1,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,2,1,0,1,0,0\">Thread 1 calls <code>P(semX)</code> -&gt; Enters critical section.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,2,1,0,1,1,0\">Thread 1 executes <code>ProcedureX()</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,2,1,0,1,2,0\">Thread 1 calls <code>P(semZ)</code> -&gt; Acquires Z.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,2,1,0,1,3,0\"><b>Crucial Step:</b> Thread 1 calls <code>V(semX)</code>. It releases the lock on X <i>before</i> it finishes its own work.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,2,1,0,1,4,0\">Thread 1 starts executing <code>ProcedureZ()</code>.</p></li></ol></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,2,1,1,0\">Now, imagine Thread 2 (also Type A) starts:</p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"5,2,1,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,2,1,1,1,0,0\">Since Thread 1 has already executed <code>V(semX)</code>, the lock is free.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,2,1,1,1,1,0\">Thread 2 calls <code>P(semX)</code> successfully and starts executing <code>ProcedureX()</code>.</p></li></ol></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,2,1,2,0\"><b>Result:</b> Thread 1 is in <code>ProcedureZ()</code> while Thread 2 is in <code>ProcedureX()</code>.</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,3,0\"><b>D. It is possible that a deadlock will occur among these threads? (FALSE)</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"5,3,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,3,1,0,0\">Deadlock requires a circular wait. Let's look at the resource hierarchy.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,3,1,1,0\">Type A requests <code>semX</code> then <code>semZ</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,3,1,2,0\">Type B requests <code>semY</code> then <code>semZ</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,3,1,3,0\">Once a thread holds <code>semZ</code>, it does not wait for any other lock (it releases <code>semX</code> or <code>semY</code> immediately).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,3,1,4,0\">Because there is no scenario where a thread holding <code>semZ</code> is forced to wait for <code>semX</code> or <code>semY</code>, a circular dependency cannot form. The system is deadlock-free.</p></li></ul></li></ul></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539745\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>A semaphore acts as a token counter: a $P()$ operation grabs a token (blocking if the count is 0), and a $V()$ operation gives a token back.</p><p><span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\">When a semaphore starts at 1, it acts as a strict mutual exclusion lock, allowing only one thread to enter a region at any time.</span></p><p><span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\">&nbsp;When a semaphore starts with a value higher than 1, it allows that specific number of threads to enter a region concurrently before blocking any others.</span></p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\">The system starts with three distinct semaphore:</p><p>$$\\text{semX} = 1, \\quad \\text{semY} = 1, \\quad \\text{semZ} = 2$$</p><ul><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\">Option A is false because any Type A thread must successfully call <code data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"67\">P(semX)</code> before it can run <code data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"93\">ProcedureX()</code>. Since <code data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"113\">semX</code> starts at 1, it functions as a strict lock, meaning two threads can never execute <code data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"200\">ProcedureX()</code> at the same time.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\">Option B is true because the path to <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"37\">ProcedureX()</code> is entirely by <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"73\">semX</code>, while the path to <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"97\">ProcedureY()</code> is entirely by <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"133\">semY</code>. Because these two sections rely on completely different locks, a Type A thread can run <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"226\">ProcedureX()</code> while a Type B thread simultaneously runs <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"281\">ProcedureY()</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\">Option C is true because a Type A thread only holds the <code data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"56\">semX</code> lock while running <code data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"80\">ProcedureX()</code>, but it drops <code data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"107\">semX</code> via <code data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"116\">V(semX)</code> before it ever enters <code data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"146\">ProcedureZ()</code>. This means one Type A thread can be inside <code data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"203\">ProcedureZ()</code> while a brand-new Type A thread grabs <code data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"254\">semX</code> and runs <code data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"268\">ProcedureX()</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\">Option D is false because a deadlock requires a circular wait condition where threads get stuck holding one resource while waiting for another. In this code, Type A threads grab <code data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\" data-index-in-node=\"178\">semX</code> then <code data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\" data-index-in-node=\"188\">semZ</code>, while Type B threads grab <code data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\" data-index-in-node=\"220\">semY</code> then <code data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\" data-index-in-node=\"230\">semZ</code>.</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\">Since no thread ever tries to grab <code data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\" data-index-in-node=\"35\">semX</code> while holding <code data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\" data-index-in-node=\"54\">semZ</code>, or grab <code data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\" data-index-in-node=\"68\">semY</code> while holding <code data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\" data-index-in-node=\"87\">semZ</code>, there is no way to form a blocking loop or circular dependency, making deadlock completely impossible.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,8,0\">Correct Options: B and C</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410203\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":9,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Assume you are given a graph that represents the relationship between four threads $\\text{(T1, T2, T3, T4)}.$ An arrow from one thread $\\text{(Tx)}$ to another $\\text{(Ty)}$ means that thread $\\text{Tx}$ must finish its computation before $\\text{Ty}$ starts. Assume that the threads can arrive in any order.</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"300\" src=\"media/9c/9c650b6755de5035ed0a.png\"></p><p>How Many MINIMUM number of counting semaphores are needed to ensure the above dependency order of execution always holds?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 16)</small></h4><a name=\"410202\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"507\" src=\"media/19/1967da7cdc87de290c9b.png\" width=\"728\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"500419\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0\">Based on the dependencies shown in the graph, the minimum number of counting semaphores required is <b>2</b>.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\">&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\">1. Analyze the Dependencies</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"3\">The graph shows a \"Diamond\" structure with two main synchronization points:</p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"4\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,0\"><b>The \"Fork\" (Fan-out):</b> Thread <b>T1</b> must finish before <b>T2</b> and <b>T3</b> can start.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,0,0\">$T1 \\rightarrow T2$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,1,0\">$T1 \\rightarrow T3$</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,1,0\"><b>The \"Join\" (Fan-in):</b> Threads <b>T2</b> and <b>T3</b> must both finish before <b>T4</b> can start.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"4,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,1,1,0,0\">$T2 \\rightarrow T4$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,1,1,1,0\">$T3 \\rightarrow T4$</p></li></ul></li></ol><p data-path-to-node=\"5\">We cannot use a single semaphore for the whole graph because the \"completion of T1\" and the \"completion of T2/T3\" are two different events that need to be tracked separately. If we mixed them, T4 might accidentally consume a signal meant for T2 or T3.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"6\">&nbsp;</p><h3>2. Semaphore 1: Handling the Fork ($S_1$)</h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"7\">We need one counting semaphore to manage the dependency where one thread enables multiple others.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"8\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"8,0,0\"><b>Initialization:</b> $S_1 = 0$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"8,1,0\"><b>T1 (Producer):</b> Since T1 has two dependents, it performs the signal operation <b>twice</b> (or increments by 2). This sets the count to 2, allowing two threads to pass.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"8,2,0\"><b>T2 &amp; T3 (Consumers):</b> Both wait on $S_1$.</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"9\"><b>Code Logic:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"10\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,0,0\"><b>T1:</b> <code>...body...; signal(S1); signal(S1);</code></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,1,0\"><b>T2:</b> <code>wait(S1); ...body...;</code></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,2,0\"><b>T3:</b> <code>wait(S1); ...body...;</code></p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"11\">&nbsp;</p><h3>3. Semaphore 2: Handling the Join ($S_2$)</h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"12\">We need a second counting semaphore to manage the dependency where one thread waits for multiple others.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"13\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"13,0,0\"><b>Initialization:</b> $S_2 = 0$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"13,1,0\"><b>T2 &amp; T3 (Producers):</b> When they finish, they each send a signal to $S_2$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"13,2,0\"><b>T4 (Consumer):</b> T4 needs to wait for <i>both</i> predecessors. It performs the wait operation <b>twice</b>. It will only proceed after the semaphore count has been incremented twice (once by T2 and once by T3).</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"14\"><b>Code Logic:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"15\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,0,0\"><b>T2:</b> <code>...body...; signal(S2);</code></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,1,0\"><b>T3:</b> <code>...body...; signal(S2);</code></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"15,2,0\"><b>T4:</b> <code>wait(S2); wait(S2); ...body...;</code></p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"16\">&nbsp;</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"17\">You need <b>1 counting semaphore</b> for the divergent dependency (Fork) and <b>1 counting semaphore</b> for the convergent dependency (Join).</p><p data-path-to-node=\"18\"><b>Total Minimum Semaphores = 2</b></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539747\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\">To minimize the number of counting semaphores used, we can group multiple dependent edges together under a single semaphore variable if they originate from the exact same parent node.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\"><span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\">visualization</span></p><p>// Shared semaphores initialized to 0<br><strong>semaphore sem1 = 0; /</strong>/ Signals from T1 to both T2 and T3<br><strong>semaphore sem2 = 0;</strong> // Signals from both T2 and T3 to T4</p><p><strong>Thread1()</strong> {<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; // Execute T1's work<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; do_work_T1();<br>&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; // Release two tokens so both T2 and T3 can unblock<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; V(sem1);<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; V(sem1);<br>}</p><p><strong>Thread2()</strong> {<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; P(sem1); // Wait for T1 to finish<br>&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; // Execute T2's work<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; do_work_T2();<br>&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; V(sem2); // Signal to T4 that T2 is done<br>}</p><p><strong>Thread3()</strong> {<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; P(sem1); // Wait for T1 to finish<br>&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; // Execute T3's work<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; do_work_T3();<br>&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; V(sem2); // Signal to T4 that T3 is done<br>}</p><p><strong>Thread4()</strong> {<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; // Wait for two tokens (one from T2, one from T3)<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; P(sem2);<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; P(sem2);<br>&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; // Execute T4's work safely<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; do_work_T4();<br>}</p><p>these threads, T2 and T3 remain blocked until T1 runs, and T4 remains blocked until both T2 and T3 finish.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410201\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":10,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider three concurrently executing threads in the same process using two semaphores $s1$ and $s2$. Assume $s1$ has been initialized to $1,$ while $s2$ has been initialized to $0.$</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"169\" width=\"746\" src=\"media/37/379a34962fb1d0f53124.png\"></p><p>What are the possible values of the global variable x, initialized to 0, after all three threads have terminated?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$x = 6$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$x = 12$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$x = 18$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$x = 36$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 5)</small></h4><a name=\"410200\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">The possible sequences are $\\text{B,C,A (x = 6) or C,A,B (x = 36) or C,B,A (x = 18).}$</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"536350\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>CS is protected by semaphore ..no data race condition<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f6/f6d10f03ce49095a4dc6.jpg\"></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410199\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":11,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">shared Integer total = 0;\nProcess A\nfor i = 1 to 3 do \n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;total = total + 1\nProcess B\nfor j = 1 to 3 do \n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;total = total + 1\n</pre><p>Assume that total must be loaded into a register before being incremented, and then is written back afterwards.<br><br>What are all the possible values that total can have when both threads complete?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$1$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$3$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$5$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$6$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 10)</small></h4><a name=\"440858\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><table border=\"3\" cellpadding=\"1\" style=\"width: 500px; margin: auto; border-spacing: 1px;\"><tbody><tr><td>Time</td><td>process-1</td><td>process-2</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Read(x) ,x=0</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Read(x) ,x=0</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Write (x) , x=1</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Read(x) ,x=1</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Write (x) , x=2</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Write (x) , x=1</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Read(x) ,x=1</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Read(x) ,x=1</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Write (x) , x=2</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Read(x) ,x=2</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Write (x) , x=3</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Write (x) , x=2</td></tr></tbody></table><p>So ,the minimum value is 2</p><table border=\"3\" cellpadding=\"1\" style=\"width: 500px; margin: auto; border-spacing: 1px;\"><tbody><tr><td>Time</td><td>process-1</td><td>process-2</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Read(x) ,x=0</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Write (x) , x=1</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Read(x) ,x=1</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Write (x) , x=2</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Read(x) ,x=2</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Write (x) , x=3</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Read(x) ,x=3</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Write (x) , x=4</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Read(x) ,x=4</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Write (x) , x=5</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Read(x) ,x=5</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Write (x) , x=6</td></tr></tbody></table><p>So ,the maximum value will be 6</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 7)</small></h4><a name=\"410198\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Possible values $: 3, 4, 5, \\text{or } 6.$ (in the worst case, in each loop, A and B both read total, then both increment, then both write, negating the effect of one of the increments).</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"502705\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>This is how 3 and 5 are also the possible values.</p><p><br><img alt=\"\" width=\"733\" height=\"400\" src=\"media/d6/d6eaac81f12baad5fecc.jpg\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539760\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\">Each increment statement </span><code data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"169\"><span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\">total = total + 1</span></code><span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\"> consists of three low-level CPU steps: loading the value from main memory into a local register, adding 1 to that register, and storing the register value back into main memory.</span></p><p>If the operating system pauses a process mid-way through these steps, its local register state becomes stale, allowing another process to perform updates that get completely wiped out when the first process wakes up and overwrites memory with its old cached value.</p><ul><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\">The system starts with a clean baseline state:</p><p>$$\\text{total} = 0$$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\">If the two processes execute completely one after another without any interruptions or preemption, each process adds exactly 3 to the counter sequentially, yielding the maximum value:</p><p>$$\\text{total} = 3 + 3 = 6$$</p></li><li><p>To get an intermediate value like 5, one process can execute its first loop cleanly to set <code data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"91\">total = 1</code>, and then get preempted right after reading <code data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"145\">total = 1</code> in its second loop. The other process then runs all 3 of its loops continuously to drive the global variable to 4, after which the first process wakes up, finishes its blocked increment to write back 2, and completes its last loop normally to reach 5.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\">To find the lowest possible result, we want the execution steps to overlap in a way that erases as many additions as possible.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\">Suppose Process A runs its very first loop, reads <code data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"50\">total = 0</code> into its local register, and is immediately paused by the CPU scheduler before it can do its math or write back.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\">While Process A is asleep with that stale value of 0 in its register, Process B wakes up and executes its first two loops cleanly one after another, successfully updating main memory to 2.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\">Right after Process B updates memory to 2, it starts its third loop, reads the value 2 into its own local register, and is immediately paused by the scheduler.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,8,0\">Now, Process A wakes up to finish its first loop; it increments its cached value of 0 to 1, writes it to main memory, erases Process B's previous updates, and then runs its remaining two loops sequentially to bring the global value up to 3.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,9,0\">Finally, Process B wakes up to finish its very last step; it increments its cached value of 2 to 3, and writes it back to main memory, which leaves the end result at 3.</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"0,9,0\">Correct Options: B, C, and D</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410197\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":12,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Here is the classic semaphore version of the producer/consumer problem:</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"525\" width=\"679\" src=\"media/a7/a7c05a9fd10c87bf2d18.png\"></p><p>For the following statements about this working solution, which statement(s) is/are TRUE?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>The semaphore empty must be initialized to $1$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The semaphore empty can be initialized to $1$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The semaphore full must be initialized to $1$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The semaphore mutex must be initialized to $1$</p>"}],"answer":["B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"410367\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1b/1b0ff6951e543971ca90.jpg\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"500418\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0\">Based on the analysis of the classic Producer-Consumer problem using semaphores, the correct statements are <b>B and D</b>.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\">Here is the step-by-step breakdown:</p><p data-path-to-node=\"2\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>1. Understand the Variables</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"3\">This code solves the \"Bounded Buffer\" problem. We have a fixed-size buffer that can hold $N$ items.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"4\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,0\"><b><code>mutex</code></b>: A binary semaphore used to ensure <b>Mutual Exclusion</b>. It protects the critical section (the actual buffer access) so that only one process (producer or consumer) can access the buffer at a time.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,1,0\"><b><code>empty</code></b>: A counting semaphore that tracks the number of <b>empty slots</b> available in the buffer.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,2,0\"><b><code>full</code></b>: A counting semaphore that tracks the number of <b>filled slots</b> (items) in the buffer.</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"5\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>2. Standard Initialization</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"6\">For a buffer of size $N$, the standard initialization is:</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"7\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,0\"><b><code>mutex = 1</code></b>: Initially, the lock is free (unlocked) so the first process can enter.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,0\"><b><code>empty = N</code></b>: Initially, the entire buffer is empty.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,2,0\"><b><code>full = 0</code></b>: Initially, there are no items in the buffer.</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"8\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>3. Evaluate the Options</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,0,0\"><b>A. The semaphore empty must be initialized to 1 (FALSE)</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,0,1,0,0\">The <code>empty</code> semaphore represents the buffer size $N$. While it <i>can</i> be 1 (if the buffer is size 1), it doesn't <i>have</i> to be. If you have a buffer of 10 slots, <code>empty</code> would be initialized to 10.</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,1,0\"><b>B. The semaphore empty can be initialized to 1 (TRUE)</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,1,1,0,0\">This is a valid configuration. If you initialize <code>empty</code> to 1, you are effectively creating a buffer with a capacity of 1 item. The code will still work correctly (it just won't be able to buffer multiple items).</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,2,0\"><b>C. The semaphore full must be initialized to 1 (FALSE)</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9,2,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,2,1,0,0\"><code>full</code> represents the number of items currently in the buffer. Since the system starts with an empty buffer, <code>full</code> must be initialized to 0. If it were initialized to 1, the consumer might successfully pass <code>sem_wait(full)</code> and try to consume an item that doesn't exist, leading to errors.</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,3,0\"><b>D. The semaphore mutex must be initialized to 1 (TRUE)</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9,3,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,3,1,0,0\">The <code>mutex</code> semaphore acts as a lock.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9,3,1,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,3,1,0,1,0,0\">If it is initialized to <b>0</b>, the very first <code>sem_wait(mutex)</code> call by anyone will block forever, causing a deadlock immediately.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,3,1,0,1,1,0\">If it is initialized to <b>&gt;1</b>, multiple processes could enter the critical section simultaneously, violating mutual exclusion (race condition).</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,3,1,1,0\">Therefore, for the lock to work, it <b>must</b> be initialized to 1.</p></li></ul></li></ul></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"499646\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Since it is tradittional bounded buffer , so empty should be initialized to n (no. of empty slots) initially</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539762\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\">Option A is false because the <code data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"30\">empty</code> semaphore tracks the total number of free slots in the shared buffer, which means it can be initialized to any positive integer capacity size depending on the system design, not strictly limited to 1.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\">Option B is true because initializing <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"38\">empty</code> to 1 simply means the system is configured with a single-slot buffer space, which is a perfectly valid&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\">Option C is false because at the very start, the shared buffer space is completely vacant, meaning there are 0 slots filled with data, so the <code data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"142\">full</code> semaphore must always be initialized to 0.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\">Option D is true because <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"25\">mutex</code> acts as a strict mutual exclusion lock to ensure that only one thread accesses the buffer array at any given moment, requiring it to start with an initial token count of 1.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410196\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":13,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>One way to avoid deadlock is to schedule threads carefully.<br>Assume the following characteristics of threads $\\text{T1, T2},$ and $\\text{T3}:$</p><ul><li>$\\text{T1}$ (at some point) acquires and releases locks $\\text{L1, L2}$</li><li>$\\text{T2}$ (at some point) acquires and releases locks $\\text{L1, L3}$</li><li>$\\text{T3}$ (at some point) acquires and releases locks $\\text{L3, L1},$ and $\\text{L4}$</li></ul><p>Which of the schedules listed below ensures that deadlock can NOT occur?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{T1}$ runs to completion, then $\\text{T2}$ to completion, then $\\text{T3}$ runs</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{T1}$ and $\\text{T2}$ run concurrently to completion, then $\\text{T3}$ runs</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{T3}$ runs to completion, then $\\text{T1}$ and $\\text{T2}$ run concurrently</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{T1}$&nbsp;and $\\text{T3}$ run concurrently to completion, then $\\text{T2}$ runs</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, C, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 5)</small></h4><a name=\"410336\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>A)Option A is correct since we are running one process after another</p>\n\n<p>B)Threads T1 and T2 can run concurrently&nbsp;&nbsp;because there is no possibility of Dead Lock</p>\n\n<p>Ex: suppose you want to run T2 and T3 concurrently then there may be a case T2 acquires lock on L1 and preempted. Then T3 acquires a lock on L3. Now the case arises where &nbsp;T2 is waiting for T3 to release a lock on L3 and T3 is waiting for T2&nbsp;to release a lock on L1. Which leads to circular dependency and leads to deadlock.<br>\nIn case T1 and T2 run concurrently if T1 acquires a lock on L1, even if it was preempted execution of T1 and execute T2 there is no chance of circular dependency as above scenario. B is also correct</p>\n\n<p>C) same as B just changes in order execution of T3 and (T1, T2)<br>\nD)T1 and T3 can execute concurrently as there no chance of Dead Lock here also.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Answer</strong><strong>)&nbsp;<em><span class=\"marker\">A,B,C,D</span></em></strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"484096\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Looking at all the given characteristics we can say that there is a possiblility of deadlock ONLY when T2 and T3 runs concurrently. Hence, none of these options will lead to a deadlock.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"500417\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0\"><b>1. Analyze the Lock Requirements</b></p><p>&nbsp;</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"3\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,0,0\"><b>T1:</b> Acquires <b>L1</b>, then <b>L2</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,1,0\"><b>T2:</b> Acquires <b>L1</b>, then <b>L3</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,2,0\"><b>T3:</b> Acquires <b>L3</b>, then <b>L1</b>, then <b>L4</b>.</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"4\"><i>(Note: We assume the list implies the order of acquisition, i.e., T3 grabs L3 first, then waits for L1).</i></p><p data-path-to-node=\"5\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>2. Identify the Deadlock Scenario</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"6\">A deadlock occurs if two threads hold a resource the other needs while waiting for the resource the other holds (a cycle).</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"7\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,0\"><b>T1 and T2:</b> Both start by requesting <b>L1</b>.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"7,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,1,0,0\">Since they compete for the <i>same</i> first lock, one will get it and the other will wait immediately.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,1,1,0\">The winner proceeds to get its second lock (L2 or L3), finishes, and releases L1.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,1,2,0\">The loser then wakes up and proceeds.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,1,3,0\"><b>Result:</b> effectively serialized (safe). <b>No Deadlock.</b></p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,0\"><b>T1 and T3:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1,0,0\">T1 acquires <b>L1</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1,1,0\">T3 acquires <b>L3</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1,2,0\"><b>Conflict Check:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1,2,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1,2,1,0,0\">T3 wants <b>L1</b> (held by T1) $\\rightarrow$ T3 waits.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1,2,1,1,0\">T1 wants <b>L2</b> (free).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1,2,1,2,0\">Does T1 want L3? <b>No.</b></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1,2,1,3,0\">Does T3 hold L2? <b>No.</b></p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1,3,0\">Since T1 does not need anything T3 holds, T1 will finish and release L1. T3 will then proceed.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,1,4,0\"><b>Result:</b> Safe Concurrency. <b>No Deadlock.</b></p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,2,0\"><b>T2 and T3:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"7,2,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,2,1,0,0\">T2 acquires <b>L1</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,2,1,1,0\">T3 acquires <b>L3</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,2,1,2,0\"><b>Conflict Check:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"7,2,1,2,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,2,1,2,1,0,0\">T2 needs <b>L3</b> (held by T3). T2 waits.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,2,1,2,1,1,0\">T3 needs <b>L1</b> (held by T2). T3 waits.</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,2,1,3,0\"><b>Result:</b> <b>DEADLOCK!</b> (Cycle: T2 $\\to$ L3 $\\to$ T3 $\\to$ L1 $\\to$ T2).</p></li></ul></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"8\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>3. Evaluate the Schedules</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"9\">To ensure deadlock does <b>NOT</b> occur, we must avoid running <b>T2 and T3</b> concurrently.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"10\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,0,0\"><b>A. T1 $\\rightarrow$ T2 $\\rightarrow$ T3:</b> Sequential. T2 and T3 never overlap. <b>Safe.</b></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,1,0\"><b>B. (T1 and T2) $\\rightarrow$ T3:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"10,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,1,1,0,0\">Phase 1: T1 and T2 run. They are safe (serialized by L1).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,1,1,1,0\">Phase 2: T3 runs alone.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,1,1,2,0\">T2 and T3 never overlap. <b>Safe.</b></p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,2,0\"><b>C. T3 $\\rightarrow$ (T1 and T2):</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"10,2,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,2,1,0,0\">Phase 1: T3 runs alone.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,2,1,1,0\">Phase 2: T1 and T2 run. Safe.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,2,1,2,0\">T2 and T3 never overlap. <b>Safe.</b></p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,3,0\"><b>D. (T1 and T3) $\\rightarrow$ T2:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"10,3,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,3,1,0,0\">Phase 1: T1 and T3 run. As analyzed above, they don't form a cycle. Safe.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,3,1,1,0\">Phase 2: T2 runs alone.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,3,1,2,0\">T2 and T3 never overlap. <b>Safe.</b></p></li></ul></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"11\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>Conclusion</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"12\">So, <b>all the schedules listed (A, B, C, and D) represent safe sequences</b> because they all successfully separate the conflicting pair (T2 and T3) in time.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"499648\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Since every option says thread runs till complition , that means they will release locks after complition , so no dead locks.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"513206\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-start=\"218\" data-end=\"651\">Option A)</p><p data-start=\"218\" data-end=\"651\">&nbsp;T1 runs to completion, acquires L1 and L2, then releases L1 and L2 upon completion. So, L1 and L2 are available. Now, T2 can acquire L1 and L3 and, upon completion, releases L1 and L3. So, L1, L2, and L3 are all available. T3 can acquire the locks now and completes execution without any deadlock.<br data-start=\"531\" data-end=\"534\">(No need of all this btw,<strong> since threads are executing sequentially, deadlock can never occur.</strong>)<br data-start=\"629\" data-end=\"632\">Thus, A is correct.</p><p data-start=\"658\" data-end=\"993\">Option&nbsp;B)</p><p data-start=\"658\" data-end=\"993\">&nbsp;T1 and T2 are executing concurrently, so focus on that only. T1 and T2 share one lock, i.e., L1. But if one of them gets it, it can execute since the other lock is not acquired by any other thread. Thus, one of T1 or T2 can execute without deadlock, then the other can execute as well when the lock is free.<br data-start=\"981\" data-end=\"984\">(Correct)</p><p data-start=\"1000\" data-end=\"1062\">Option C)</p><p data-start=\"1000\" data-end=\"1062\">&nbsp;It’s just the reverse of option B.<br data-start=\"1050\" data-end=\"1053\">(Correct)</p><p data-start=\"1069\" data-end=\"1393\">Option D)</p><p data-start=\"1069\" data-end=\"1393\">&nbsp;Similar explanation as option A, only L1 is the common lock between T1 and T3. If any one of them gets L1, it doesn’t have to wait for other locks because they are not acquired by any other thread. So any one of T1 or T3 can execute, then the other, and at last T2 can execute.<br data-start=\"1362\" data-end=\"1365\">So option D is also correct.</p><p data-start=\"1400\" data-end=\"1459\">Hence, A, B, C, D all are correct.&nbsp;<br data-start=\"1442\" data-end=\"1445\">Hope it helps.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 6 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539763\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\">This problem focuses on deadlock avoidance through scheduling, where we look at the specific conditions that cause threads to get permanently stuck.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\"><span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\">&nbsp;A deadlock can only happen if four conditions are met at the same time: mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, and circular wait.&nbsp;</span></p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\">The most common trigger is circular wait, which happens when Thread A holds Lock 1 and waits for Lock 2, while Thread B holds Lock 2 and waits for Lock 1.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\">&nbsp;If we can schedule threads so that they never run concurrently while sharing locks, we break the possibility of overlapping resource requests and completely eliminate deadlocks.</p><ul><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\">Option A is true because running the threads strictly one after another (T1 $\\rightarrow$ T2 $\\rightarrow$ T3) means only one thread is active at any time, which completely removes any chance of overlapping lock requests or circular waiting.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\">Option B is true because we need to check if T1 and T2 can deadlock when running together. T1 needs L1 and L2, while T2 needs L1 and L3; since both threads require L1 as their primary shared constraint, whoever grabs L1 first will run to completion while the other safely waits, meaning they cannot form a circular deadlock loop before T3 runs.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\">Option C is true because running T3 completely to the end first frees up all its held locks (L3, L1, L4). When T1 and T2 run together afterward, they only compete over L1, which we already established cannot cause a deadlock.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\">Option D is true because we need to check if T1 and T3 can deadlock when running concurrently. T1 requires L1 and L2, while T3 requires L3, L1, and L4; since their only shared lock is L1, one thread will acquire L1 and finish its task, leaving no way for a cross-lock dependency to develop before T2 runs.</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\">Correct Options: A, B, C, and D</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410195\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":14,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Given a basic spin lock, assume that locking the spin lock takes $\\text{A}$ time units (if no one is holding the lock); unlock also takes $\\text{A}$ time units. Furthermore, let $\\text{C}$ represent the time it takes for a context switch, and $\\text{T}$ be the maximum time any thread can spend in busy wait before yielding the CPU.<br>That means if a thread is doing a busy wait then it can wait for a maximum of&nbsp;$\\text{T}$ time after that it gives up the CPU voluntarily.</p><p><br>Assume this code sequence, executed by two threads on one processor at roughly the same time:</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"115\" width=\"593\" src=\"media/22/2253071b06e664a5f1ac.png\"></p><p>What is the worst-case time for the two threads to finish this code sequence? Assume that only three context switches can occur at a maximum.</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{4 A+C}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{4 A+3 C}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{4 A+C+3 T}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{4 A+3 C+T}$</p>"}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 14)</small></h4><a name=\"410371\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><ol>\n\t<li>T1 acquires lock&nbsp;(Time taken: $\\text{A}$ )<br>\n\t&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>Suppose a context switch happens&nbsp; (Time taken: $\\text{C}$ )<br>\n\t&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>T2&nbsp;TRIES to acquire lock but will do a busy wait for a maximum of&nbsp;$\\text{T}$ time<br>\n\t&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>A context switch happens&nbsp;&nbsp;(Time taken: $\\text{C}$ )<br>\n\t&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>T1 releases the lock&nbsp;(Time taken: $\\text{A}$ )<br>\n\t&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>Suppose a context switch happens&nbsp;&nbsp;(Time taken: $\\text{C}$ )<br>\n\t&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>T2 get the lock&nbsp;(Time taken: $\\text{A}$ )<br>\n\t&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>T2&nbsp;releases the lock&nbsp;(Time taken: $\\text{A}$ )</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><br>\nTotal Time =&nbsp;$\\text{A} + \\text{C} + \\text{T} + \\text{C} + \\text{A} + \\text{C} + \\text{A} + \\text{A} = 4\\text{A}+3\\text{C}+\\text{T}$</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/94/94247559383cf65c5d28.jpg\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"412545\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>The goal :</strong>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong>(1) Context switch : </strong>Perform as many context switches as you can. We can perform three context switches at most, Time taken = 3C</p>\n\n<p><strong>(2) Busy wait :</strong>&nbsp; Spend as much time in busy wait as you can, Just T as we have to give up after that and you will be forced to perform a context switch. So with a context switch limit of 3, you can only schedule the waiting thread only once.</p>\n\n<p>T1(Who initially acquired the lock) →&nbsp;T2 (Busy wait for&nbsp; T unit of time)→&nbsp;T1 →&nbsp;T2</p>\n\n<p><strong>(3) Other operations :</strong> The cost of rest of the operations is 4A, acquire the lock twice, release the lock twice.</p>\n\n<p>Max total time = 4A + 3C + T</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"410318\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">What is the use of time slice here? <br>\nMy answer was 4A + 3C</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"500416\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0\">Based on the detailed timeline of events required to create the \"worst-case\" scenario, the correct answer is <b>D. $4 A + 3 C + T$</b>.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\">Here is the step-by-step breakdown of how this timing is calculated.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"2\">&nbsp;</p><h3>## Understanding the Goal</h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"3\">To find the <b>worst-case time</b>, we need to construct a scenario where the most time-consuming events happen:</p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"4\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,0\">Both threads need to run their lock/unlock code ($4 \\times A$ total).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,1,0\">We need to trigger the maximum allowed context switches ($3 \\times C$).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,2,0\">We need to force a thread to busy-wait for the maximum allowed time ($T$).</p></li></ol><p data-path-to-node=\"5\">&nbsp;</p><h3>## The Worst-Case Timeline</h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"6\">Let's assume two threads, <b>Thread 1 (T1)</b> and <b>Thread 2 (T2)</b>.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"7\"><b>1. T1 Acquires the Lock</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"8\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"8,0,0\">T1 starts and executes <code>mutex_lock()</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"8,1,0\"><b>Time:</b> $A$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"8,2,0\"><i>Worst-Case Event:</i> Immediately after getting the lock (but before unlocking), T1 is preempted (interrupted) by the scheduler. This forces the <b>1st Context Switch</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"8,3,0\"><b>Time:</b> $C$</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"9\"><b>2. T2 Tries to Acquire the Lock (The Busy Wait)</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"10\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,0,0\">The CPU is now given to T2.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,1,0\">T2 tries to run <code>mutex_lock()</code>, but T1 still holds the lock.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,2,0\">T2 enters a \"busy wait\" loop.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,3,0\">According to the problem, it waits for a maximum of $T$ time before giving up.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,4,0\"><b>Time:</b> $T$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,5,0\">After the timeout, T2 voluntarily yields the CPU, causing the <b>2nd Context Switch</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,6,0\"><b>Time:</b> $C$</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"11\"><b>3. T1 Finishes and Exits</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"12\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,0,0\">The CPU is back with T1.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,1,0\">T1 executes <code>mutex_unlock()</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,2,0\"><b>Time:</b> $A$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,3,0\">T1 exits (0 time). Since T1 is done, the CPU must switch to the remaining thread (T2). This is the <b>3rd Context Switch</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,4,0\"><b>Time:</b> $C$</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"13\"><b>4. T2 Finally Runs Successfully</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"14\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,0,0\">The CPU is back with T2.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,0\">T2 executes <code>mutex_lock()</code> (The lock is free now).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,2,0\"><b>Time:</b> $A$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,3,0\">T2 executes <code>mutex_unlock()</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,4,0\"><b>Time:</b> $A$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,5,0\">T2 exits.</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"15\">&nbsp;</p><h3>## Calculation</h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"16\">Now, sum up all the time components from the steps above:</p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"17\"><li><p>Lock/Unlock Operations: T1 Lock + T1 Unlock + T2 Lock + T2 Unlock</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$A + A + A + A = 4A$$</p></li><li><p>Context Switches: Switch (T1$\\to$T2) + Switch (T2$\\to$T1) + Switch (T1$\\to$T2)</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$C + C + C = 3C$$</p></li><li><p>Busy Wait: T2 wasting time waiting for the lock</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$T$$</p></li></ol><p>Total Worst-Case Time:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$4A + 3C + T$$</p><p data-path-to-node=\"20\">This matches <b>Option D</b>.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"530400\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><h3 data-path-to-node=\"2\">1. Thread 1 Starts (Locking)</h3><ul data-path-to-node=\"3\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,0,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"3,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Thread 1</b> begins and acquires the lock. According to the problem, this takes <b data-path-to-node=\"3,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"76\">$A$</b> time units.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,1,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"3,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Cumulative Time: $A$</b></p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=\"4\">2. Preemption (The Worst Timing)</h3><ul data-path-to-node=\"5\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,0\">Just as Thread 1 enters the critical section (having acquired the lock), a <b data-path-to-node=\"5,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"75\">context switch</b> occurs. This takes <b data-path-to-node=\"5,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"109\">$C$</b> time units.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"5,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Thread 2</b> is now scheduled to run.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,2,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"5,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Cumulative Time: $A + C$</b></p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=\"6\">3. Thread 2 Busy-Waits</h3><ul data-path-to-node=\"7\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,0\">Thread 2 tries to call <code data-path-to-node=\"7,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"23\">mutex_lock()</code>. Since Thread 1 still holds the lock, Thread 2 must busy-wait.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,0\">The problem states a thread can busy-wait for a maximum of <b data-path-to-node=\"7,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"59\">$T$</b> time before yielding the CPU. In the worst case, Thread 2 uses that entire time <b data-path-to-node=\"7,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"141\">$T$</b> hoping the lock will be released.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,2,0\">After time $T$ expires, another <b data-path-to-node=\"7,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"30\">context switch</b> occurs to put Thread 1 back on the CPU. This costs another <b data-path-to-node=\"7,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"104\">$C$</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"7,3,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"7,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Cumulative Time: $A + 2C + T$</b></p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=\"8\">4. Thread 1 Finishes</h3><ul data-path-to-node=\"9\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,0,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"9,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Thread 1</b> resumes and completes its work (which takes no time) and then calls <code data-path-to-node=\"9,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"77\">mutex_unlock()</code>. Unlocking takes <b data-path-to-node=\"9,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"109\">$A$</b> time units.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,1,0\">Thread 1 then exits. To let Thread 2 finish, a final <b data-path-to-node=\"9,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"53\">context switch</b> occurs, costing <b data-path-to-node=\"9,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"84\">$C$</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,2,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"9,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Cumulative Time: $2A + 3C + T$</b></p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=\"10\">5. Thread 2 Finally Executes</h3><ul data-path-to-node=\"11\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"11,0,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"11,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Thread 2</b> is back on the CPU. It must now acquire the lock itself, which takes <b data-path-to-node=\"11,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"78\">$A$</b> time units.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"11,1,0\">It does its work (no time) and then releases the lock, which takes another <b data-path-to-node=\"11,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"75\">$A$</b> time units.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"11,2,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"11,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Cumulative Time: $4A + 3C + T$</b></p></li></ul></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 6 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539765\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\">In a single-core system, a spinning thread cannot make progress until the operating system performs a context switch—which saves the current thread's state and loads another—allowing the lock holder to run and release it.&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"0,0,0\">To prevent complete CPU waste, hybrid spinlocks use a timeout limit $T$: the thread spins for up to $T$ time units, and if the lock is still busy, it voluntarily yields the CPU, triggering a context switch.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"0\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\">Step 1: Thread 1 starts running and calls <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"42\">mutex_lock()</code>. Since the lock is totally free, acquiring it takes exactly $A$ time units.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\">Step 2: Right after Thread 1 gets the lock but before it can unlock it, a timer interrupt occurs, forcing the first context switch. This switch takes $C$ time units and puts Thread 2 on the CPU.</p><p>$$\\text{Current Time} = A + C$$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\">Step 3: Thread 2 tries to call <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"31\">mutex_lock()</code>, but it finds that Thread 1 already holds it. Thread 2 enters a busy-wait cycle, spinning for the absolute maximum allowed time limit $T$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\">Step 4: After wasting $T$ time units spinning, Thread 2 voluntarily gives up the CPU. This triggers the second context switch, taking another $C$ time units to bring Thread 1 back.</p><p>$$\\text{Current Time} = (A + C) + T + C = A + 2C + T$$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\">Step 5: Thread 1 wakes up and resumes exactly where it left off. It runs <code data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\" data-index-in-node=\"73\">do_something()</code> (0 time units) and then executes <code data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\" data-index-in-node=\"121\">mutex_unlock()</code>, which takes $A$ time units to complete.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\">Step 6: Thread 1 terminates by calling <code data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\" data-index-in-node=\"39\">exit()</code> (0 time units). Because Thread 1 is done, the operating system executes the third and final context switch to pass control back to Thread 2, taking $C$ time units.</p><p>$$\\text{Current Time} = (A + 2C + T) + A + C = 2A + 3C + T$$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,8,0\">Step 7: Thread 2 wakes up and retries its <code data-path-to-node=\"0,8,0\" data-index-in-node=\"42\">mutex_lock()</code> operation. Since Thread 1 successfully freed the lock earlier, Thread 2 easily acquires it in $A$ time units.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,9,0\">Step 8: Thread 2 completes its execution by running <code data-path-to-node=\"0,9,0\" data-index-in-node=\"52\">do_something()</code> (0 time units), releasing the lock with <code data-path-to-node=\"0,9,0\" data-index-in-node=\"107\">mutex_unlock()</code> ($A$ time units), and calling <code data-path-to-node=\"0,9,0\" data-index-in-node=\"150\">exit()</code> (0 time units).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,10,0\">Summing up all the individual time units spent across this sequence gives us the total worst-case execution time:</p><p>$$\\text{Total Time} = (2A + 3C + T) + A + A = 4A + 3C + T$$</p></li></ul></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410194\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":15,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Assume you are building a special operating system that requires executing the expression:</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">z = F3(F1(x), F2(F3(y)));</pre><p>where $x, y,$&nbsp;and $z$ are shared integers and $\\text{F1, F2, F3}$ are functions.<br>Our job is to write the synchronization code to force the expression to be evaluated regardless of the order they are run by the CPU scheduler.</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"345\" width=\"565\" src=\"media/51/5145ef537359cb3cefb2.png\"></p><p>What will be the minimum number of binary semaphores to make sure of this?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2,2],"answerText":"2","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 22)</small></h4><a name=\"410342\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>We need minimum&nbsp;two binary semaphores P&nbsp;and Q, where initially P = 0 and Q = 0</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/22/22cd92adafdbbbe78be3.png\" style=\"float:left\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/72/72b73ec5e2b6981fdeeb.png\" style=\"float:left\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 7)</small></h4><a name=\"412541\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>In order to get the value of the expression similar to the question,</p>\n\n<p>- F3(y) must be calculated before F2(y). Line 1 must be executed before line 2</p>\n\n<p>- F2(y) must be calculated before z. Line 2&nbsp;must be executed before line 3</p>\n\n<p>We need at least two binary&nbsp;semaphores to ensure the above. The implementation is as follows</p>\n\n<p>Initialize two binary semaphores p, q = 0</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f0/f079ed5d7fd706192774.jpg\" width=\"5000\"></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong>Answer</strong> :&nbsp;2</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410193\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":16,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>&nbsp;Consider a system that has many threads of two difference types (typeA and typeB). All the threads call the below subroutine named <em>routine</em> passing their type as an argument.</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"394\" width=\"762\" src=\"media/53/539ffbb412c2f86df354.png\"></p><p>Let '$a$' represent the number of typeA threads that pass through the $\\textsf{'DoIt()'}$ function, and '$b$' represent the number of typeB threads that pass through the 'DoIt()' function. What relationship must ALWAYS hold true at any point in the system?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$a &lt; 2b$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$b &lt; 2a$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$b \\leq a \\leq 2b$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$a \\leq b \\leq 2a$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 8)</small></h4><a name=\"410433\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Note: A thread is in $DoIt()$ section, if it has executed the last instruction of it's respected $if$ part.<br>\n<br>\n$semaX$ and $semaY$ is both initialized to 0, meaning that initially both $semaX$ and $semaY$ are locked.<br>\n<br>\nInitially Type A threads will wait at $P(semaX$), until a Type B thread will signal $V(semaX)$.<br>\n<br>\nFor every Type B thread, two Type A threads can pass through $DoIt()$. i.e $a \\le 2b$<br>\n<br>\nSo, Initially one or two Type A threads can pass through $DoIt()$ even before any Type B threads can pass through $DoIt()$, Therefore a = 1 or 2 when b = 0 and at this point the value of semaphores are $semaX = 0$, $semaY = 2$<br>\n<br>\nNow after this, We can observe that, if new type A threads arrives it will wait until a new type B threads arrives and signal’s $V(semaX)$ allowing for more type A threads to pass through $DoIt()$ i.e holding the relation $b \\le a$<br>\n<br>\nOR<br>\n<br>\ntwo Type B threads can pass through $DoIt()$, till type A waits at $P(semaX)$, i.e a = 2, b = 2.<br>\n<br>\nTherefore A, B, D are incorrect. Only relation C holds.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"500423\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"12\"><b>Correct Answer: C. b≤a≤2b</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"13\">We can derive this by analyzing the producer-consumer relationships between the two thread types.</p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"14\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,0,0\"><b>Dependency 1: Bs need As (b≤a)</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"14,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,0,1,0,0\">To finish, a Type B thread must pass <code>P(semY)</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,0,1,1,0\"><code>semY</code> is produced by Type A threads calling <code>V(semY)</code>. Each completed Type A thread produces <b>1</b> unit of Y.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,0,1,2,0\">Therefore, the number of completed B threads (b) cannot exceed the number of available Ys (a).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,0,1,3,0\"><b>Result:</b> b≤a.</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,0\"><b>Dependency 2: As need Bs (a≤2b)</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"14,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,1,0,0\">To finish, a Type A thread must pass <code>P(semX)</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,1,1,0\"><code>semX</code> is produced by Type B threads calling <code>V(semX)</code>. Crucially, each Type B thread produces <b>2</b> units of X (calling V twice).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,1,2,0\">This means 1 started Type B thread can support 2 Type A threads.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,1,3,0\">Let NB​ be the number of Type B threads that have started. Then a≤2⋅NB​.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,1,4,0\">However, if there are enough As to consume those Xs, they will produce Ys, allowing the waiting Bs to finish. In a stable system where threads progress if possible, the only reason a started B thread wouldn't finish is if it's waiting for a Y. But if a is large (close to 2⋅NB​), there are plenty of Ys.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,1,5,0\">Essentially, if a grows large, it \"unlocks\" the Bs, forcing b to catch up. a cannot simply run away to infinity; it is tethered to the production from b.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,1,6,0\">Since each b provides the resources for 2 a's, the number of a's cannot exceed twice the number of b's.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,1,7,0\"><b>Result:</b> a≤2b.</p></li></ul></li></ol><p data-path-to-node=\"15\"><b>Combined Relationship:</b> b≤a≤2b.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539769\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\">Let us analyze the lifecycle of a <b data-path-to-node=\"0,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"34\">typeA</b> thread:</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"0,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,1,1,0,0\">It must consume 1 token from <code data-path-to-node=\"0,1,1,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"29\">semaX</code> via <code data-path-to-node=\"0,1,1,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"39\">P(semaX)</code> to proceed.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,1,1,1,0\">Right after passing, it produces 1 token for <code data-path-to-node=\"0,1,1,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"45\">semaY</code> via <code data-path-to-node=\"0,1,1,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"55\">V(semaY)</code> and enters <code data-path-to-node=\"0,1,1,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"75\">DoIt()</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,1,1,2,0\">Therefore, the number of tokens added to <code data-path-to-node=\"0,1,1,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"41\">semaY</code> is equal to the number of typeA threads that pass, which is $a$.</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\">Let us analyze the lifecycle of a <b data-path-to-node=\"0,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"34\">typeB</b> thread:</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"0,2,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,2,1,0,0\">It starts by producing 2 tokens for <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,1,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"36\">semaX</code> via two consecutive <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,1,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"62\">V(semaX)</code> calls.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,2,1,1,0\">It then must consume 1 token from <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,1,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"34\">semaY</code> via <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,1,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"44\">P(semaY)</code> before it can enter <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,1,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"73\">DoIt()</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,2,1,2,0\">Therefore, to let $b$ number of typeB threads reach <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,1,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"50\">DoIt()</code>, they must successfully clear <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,1,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"87\">P(semaY)</code>, which requires at least $b$ tokens from <code data-path-to-node=\"0,2,1,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"135\">semaY</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\">Since tokens on <code data-path-to-node=\"0,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"16\">semaY</code> are exclusively created by typeA threads, the number of consumed tokens ($b$) can never exceed the total generated tokens ($a$):</p><p>$$b \\leq a$$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\">Now let us look at <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"19\">semaX</code>. Each typeA thread consumes 1 token from <code data-path-to-node=\"0,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"66\">semaX</code>. To have $a$ threads pass, they must consume $a$ tokens.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\">These tokens are exclusively generated by typeB threads, where each typeB thread contributes exactly 2 tokens to <code data-path-to-node=\"0,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"113\">semaX</code> before it even reaches its own gate.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\">If $b$ threads have passed <code data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\" data-index-in-node=\"25\">DoIt()</code>, they have already deposited $2b$ tokens into <code data-path-to-node=\"0,6,0\" data-index-in-node=\"76\">semaX</code>. Thus, the maximum number of typeA threads that could possibly pass cannot exceed this generated pool:</p><p>$$a \\leq 2b$$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"0,7,0\">Combining both directional boundaries</p><p>$$b \\leq a \\leq 2b$$</p></li></ul></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/410192\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"}],"sectionsMeta":[{"name":"Technical","marks":25.0,"instructions":null}],"math":true,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"Technical","start":0,"count":16}],"stats":{"questions":16,"maxMarks":18,"types":{"MSQ":11,"MCQ":2,"NAT":3},"withoutKey":4}};
